/**
 * @struct crypto_policy_t
   * @brief The inputs used to resolve the connection's security mode: whether the transport is already secure, whether encryption or authentication is required or disabled, and which password, identity, and verification-key material is available. The resolver reads this value and writes the resulting policy into the security context; it does not own any key or password storage.
 */
/**
 * @struct crypto_record
   * @brief Binds a live crypto context to record-layer state. It tracks the current and previous key epochs, which old flows may still accept, the delivery class of the last received record, datagram replay observations, and whether this endpoint is the server. During rekeying the previous context stays here until every allowed flow has retired.
 */
/**
 * @struct crypto_rekey_session_t
   * @brief Coordinates one in-flight session-key rotation. It points at the record state and packet-emission callback, remembers the peer's acknowledged epoch, holds a deferred peer key while rate limits permit processing it, and records whether requests, markers, and acknowledgements are outstanding. The deferred key is wiped when the exchange is consumed.
 */
/**
 * @struct crypto_session
   * @brief Shared, mutex-protected record and rekey state attached to one `crypto_context_t`. It owns the record state, rotation state, and bounded queue of control messages emitted by rekeying; `count` tracks queued messages and `failure` latches the first terminal crypto error until the session is destroyed.
 */
/**
 * @struct crypto_session_message_t
   * @brief One queued rekey control message. `channel` selects the ACIP flow, `type` identifies the marker, request, response, acknowledgement, or needed signal, and `length` plus the inline `payload` hold the bounded key or epoch data copied into the queue.
 */
